[{"id":"VRCE-4-58-1","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":1,"english":"Then hearing that ambrosial speech of the vulture-king, the monkey-chiefs, being delighted, kept on parleying (on that topic).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.867,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058001","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9e83b6756055e62093534f90027c30b142492eb7c60cc05ed5a3810cf7c9f2b3","compact":"084cd87709953a16406f5fe7c5a91f7db11131d1a2a9ae56df120ca061bee01c","nasal":"084cd87709953a16406f5fe7c5a91f7db11131d1a2a9ae56df120ca061bee01c"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-2","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":2,"english":"Then Jāmbavān-foremost of monkeystogether with all the monkeys, arising suddenly from the ground, spoke to the vulture-king, saying,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.712,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058002","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b11d2ea242d5c42b5791230cb352b7b76c76f7e1e221d467ff5dbb4998ad18de","compact":"9ad545ba1223b8ea66a45035b0fc5442c033453b44cb106d779ac086c59a9ca9","nasal":"22c5b129e1c0686fdfa759f903ab58e7d19b62f554ececd3cb12140737cbcd55"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-3","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":3,"english":"Where is Sītā? By whom has she been seen? And who has carried away Mithila's daughter? Do you tell us all about this, and (thus) prove the path of these rangers of the woods.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.885,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058003","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"421e1091a59f05cb3569f751105538ff4473447a7089abd0cd8fc591009d8076","compact":"5071f1b7873c2b151756b662095abc33c916f3425ed956a427dc54c6f85772b4","nasal":"5071f1b7873c2b151756b662095abc33c916f3425ed956a427dc54c6f85772b4"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-4","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":4,"english":"Who is there that does not take heed of the force of Dasarathi's* and Laksmana's arrows, speeding with the vehemence of thunder? *Lit. Dasaratha's son. The term is applied in especial to Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058004","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4744bf1f23ff3fe1c2b3aa70056b43575b4fc4a2d46f5f24a89c790350016077","compact":"cb930c2023c5594f9ebc7c2dbbd82a61d2e7491c34b8ec3632ca781c2d4c0b16","nasal":"dfffce8981bbff5fdd7f9a383c755431a481dc4667f9287cc81429f3d416c5c5"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-5","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":5,"english":"Thereat, cheering up those monkeys, who had risen from their attitudes of fasting, and who were all attention to hear news concerning Sītā, he (Sampāti), well pleased, said these words,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.803,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058005","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bfc50b9fbfbd2a31a5b37f2e95d2a9eed069ffa61c4813853fdbaa52690f03fa","compact":"8559ea737641c35223816ebc31f77a8e9663a8ab45345588a7bb577360ea6358","nasal":"8559ea737641c35223816ebc31f77a8e9663a8ab45345588a7bb577360ea6358"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-6","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":6,"english":"Listen as to how I came to know of the ravishment of Vaidehī, Sītā, at this place; and who it was that told me where that one of expansive eyes is.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058006","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"33313be28ba6f6dd453c9367e89539107c92645db940cf1153bb4eb9689a56c9","compact":"b43d6544eea3e6af07a0414e267289bd266ebaa92e0efc5b8911e42ee02bc258","nasal":"b43d6544eea3e6af07a0414e267289bd266ebaa92e0efc5b8911e42ee02bc258"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-7","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":7,"english":"For a long time I lay here, old, and of feeble life and energy. that best of birds, my son named Supārswa, coming to me, at the proper hour maintained me with food.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058007","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c9737fae145ef5738586445a9102e5532a91e37356b05d23e4abfc0c38ce673d","compact":"3de7f4c1188d76bcde7ea8416345ae8636b046e7e9ba04f452a28338b5673bfa","nasal":"d11fff9c47f63c35b1a9fee09d9ef47c32d0fd7a27c0de9d5a3f7717c06adbfc"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-8","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":8,"english":"For a long time I lay here, old, and of feeble life and energy. that best of birds, my son named Supārswa, coming to me, at the proper hour maintained me with food.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.536,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058008","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0ded22f9b7e733400aed5f54f04d65581024656bcd8a836787717600a89df869","compact":"aeda4607f8097a97fd324f8d6c1e7adcf140e88961449bb7cd3abe10cf322743","nasal":"aeda4607f8097a97fd324f8d6c1e7adcf140e88961449bb7cd3abe10cf322743"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-9","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":9,"english":"Gandharvas exceedingly lascivious; serpents are exceedingly wrathful; fear is excessive in deer; and we have excessive hunger.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.897,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058009","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"40a15e9bbb48e89290ea30a6eeaaf13a06b81928f48c894769a860dd4f4e3b8c","compact":"885597f68aff9e1b187359d6c3749252e954b8adbb87b0c1b9eecd4b122aa203","nasal":"8ce216f829008f524c8182d4d32d4fdf6f4e2696cc8abd3937bcde92e7888961"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-10","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":10,"english":"Once on a time my son, going forth about sunrise to procure food for me who was suffering from hunger and (accordingly) eager for it, came back in the evening without any flesh. are","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.697,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058010","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ac65ca5acf6dc5ca3b427df9b9089a79de81a3a0dbb8ff54609c2067c7774e21","compact":"81177a8095f8c89c712c66ed5b4f63494769db49793d17f6d3ebdd47c9fced65","nasal":"d215c27472839b206fd349cdede7134368e773b28cf7eb42b4944a30c29be117"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-11","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":11,"english":"And each apart remaining at no great distance from the others, Gaja, Gavākha and Gavaya, Sarabha, Gandhamādana, Mainda, Dwivida, and Hanumān, Jambavān, the youthful prince Angada, and Tārā, remaining in sight of the wood, after having searched the South-lands covered with mountain ranges, were searching (about), when they espied an unenclosed cavity, difficult of entry, named Věkşavila, guarded by a Dānava. And tried by hunger and thirst, worn out with fatigue, seeking for water, they found that cavity surrounded by trees and plants. And, with their bodies drenched and reddened with lotus dust, Kraunchas and swans and cranes and Chakravākas, came out of the cavity.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.15,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-50-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058011","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8c0889298793926193c40c9fab6047ea6e823c572fa01266158d7de0d0f873d3","compact":"42a65625a570209094e17cad961ffb6bb1a7ea3bd2bac2e8f1dafcb5ce4720c4","nasal":"42a65625a570209094e17cad961ffb6bb1a7ea3bd2bac2e8f1dafcb5ce4720c4"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-12","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":12,"english":"Hurt (by my speech uttered) in consequence of my non-receipt of food, that enhancer of my joy, asking my forgiveness, said these words fraught with fact,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.828,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058012","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3871300b7c43b73dd68dc332b86762784d68bdc07dfe5ada31cc79749e5bd1b8","compact":"1780abf5a04e24c646c0be08e944f0543beba250563bfa4af4c2216e95dd9442","nasal":"1780abf5a04e24c646c0be08e944f0543beba250563bfa4af4c2216e95dd9442"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-13","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":13,"english":"'Father, taking to my wings at the proper time for procuring flesh, I (went) and stood, obstructing the pass of the Mahendra mountain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.651,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058013","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4eb65e9d0e70d323c28e92830940fbf47c83c1a5157fd9e9788ae7d244b99754","compact":"137afb3618f62151b9cbb273d217da36f3c18abe9077fd5f74e4c87aec2baddf","nasal":"c5cabde002bbd655fbbe71fa133d01cc35dd8b38bca9f6c6b955944ee3f12327"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-14","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":14,"english":"There stood I looking down, obstructing the way of thousands of creatures ranging the sea.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058014","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"485c4dba4051906519af0891c533e1ae037d55e614be436bbc323acde6838462","compact":"0efe82b0b00bd44754a4ad437239359ce0d3d0abfab0f9e47bf44df069e3a845","nasal":"f8330164a19040d17e735beedc09f32521c763cecf5432a9e2b415190c8d9d79"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-15","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":15,"english":"There I saw somebody resembling a mass of crushed collyrium, going away, taking a female resembling the rising sun in splendour.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058015","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2e58ad14e47cd974262a50289fd54934f7f276231faeb2c1758cf81bf965b705","compact":"640209ae6e78d244d34d6050c55114db49f433d81bba2927f0052bda224378d5","nasal":"23c7aa058fb2549e0e4a2b43fed1e2e487f3ecf66cb4784b91baf50f285d38f9"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-58-16","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":58,"verse":16,"english":"Seeing them I had made up my mind that they should serve for your fare, when he humbly in a pacific manner begged for way.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.733,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-59-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4058016","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7624720e15606fb502ed28cd56e8f9e3d656009dc5553f4933d6b3b4c84a2a61","compact":"0843ce77a1d2be34e382dc9e527ba3163d5702d8ffbf0e0518a5ae054